{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and one of the most influential nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. He was born in 1140 the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II which gave him strong royal connections. Raymond inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Because of his youth a regency was established with his mother and his uncle Baldwin III acting as guardians. He was formally crowned in 1157 and began to assert his own authority over the county. His early rule was marked by efforts to stabilize the region and strengthen ties with neighboring Christian states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became deeply involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a frequent rival of the king and often opposed the policies of the royal court. He clashed with King Amalric I and later with Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was named regent for Baldwin IV in 1174 but was removed from the position in 1176 due to his perceived incompetence and lack of decisive leadership. His rivalry with the king intensified when he was accused of conspiring with Saladin the Muslim leader who was uniting the Islamic world against the Crusader states. Raymond denied these accusations but his reputation suffered greatly. Despite his political missteps he remained a powerful figure in the region and maintained control over Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who urged caution against engaging Saladin in open battle. He warned that the Crusader army was overextended and vulnerable. His advice was ignored and the army was crushed at Hattin. Raymond himself was not present at the battle but he was captured shortly after by Saladin. He was held in captivity for several months and then released. After his release he returned to Tripoli and spent his final years trying to rebuild the county. He died in 1187 shortly after his return. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli as it entered a period of decline. Raymond III remains a complex figure in Crusader history remembered for his ambition his political maneuvering and his tragic failures.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.